653 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/A<</O/Layout/Placement/Block>>/Alt(Risk factors for AHAI are illustrated against a mountain backdrop. Factors include history of altitude illness, respiratory and cardiovascular diseases, anxiety, and obesity. A graph shows altitude affecting these risks, with markers for HAPE, HACE, and AMS at various altitudes. Age, sex, and racial differences are additional considerations.)/K 672 0 R/P 224 0 R/Pg 213 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

654 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/A<</O/Layout/Placement/Block>>/Alt(Diagram illustrating the effects of hypoxia at high altitudes on the human body, featuring blood-brain barrier disruption, increased cerebral and capillary pressure, and various physiological responses, including pulmonary vasoconstriction and hypertension. The process involves AMS \(Acute Mountain Sickness\), HACE \(High Altitude Cerebral Edema\), and HAPE \(High Altitude Pulmonary Edema\), with background imagery of mountains. Arrows indicate flow and interaction between different conditions and body parts.)/K 673 0 R/P 224 0 R/Pg 217 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

655 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/A<</O/Layout/Placement/Block>>/Alt(Circular graphic depicting molecular structures of drugs used for Acute High-Altitude Illness \(AHAI\) with a central image of a mountain. Drugs include Albuterol, Tadalafil, Sildenafil, Salmeterol, Furosemide, Acetazolamide, Dexamethasone, Nifedipine, Ibuprofen, and Acetaminophen, displayed around the mountain labeled with AMS, HACE, and HAPE.)/K 674 0 R/P 224 0 R/Pg 219 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

